---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import GzipExample from '../../components/GzipExample.astro';
import { guideBySlug } from '../../lib/guides';
import { gzipExamples } from '../../lib/gzip-examples';

const guide = guideBySlug('decode-base64-gzip-to-json');
const example = gzipExamples[0];
---

<GuideLayout guide={guide} keywords="decode base64 gzip to JSON, base64 gzip decoder, gzip base64 example, H4sI decode, decompress API payload">
  <p>You copy a value from an API response or log, paste it into a Base64 decoder, and get unreadable characters. The value may be Base64-encoded <em>compressed data</em>, not Base64-encoded text. If the sender used GZip, there is one more step before you can read it.</p>

  <blockquote><strong>The decoding order:</strong> Base64 string → compressed bytes → GZip decompression → UTF-8 text. Parse or format the JSON only after those steps.</blockquote>

  <h2>A complete Base64 GZip example</h2>
  <p>This small order-status message is synthetic. The link opens the <a href="/gzip-base64/">GZip Base64 decoder</a> with the input and decompression action already selected, so you can compare the result without assembling a test yourself.</p>
  <GzipExample example={example} />

  <h2>Decode your own payload in the browser</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Copy the complete Base64 string. A quoted JSON string is accepted, but the key, comma, or entire response object is not part of the encoded value.</li>
    <li>Open the decoder and replace Input with that value.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>Decode & Decompress</strong>.</li>
    <li>Compare Output with what the producer says it sent. Use <strong>Copy Output</strong>, <strong>Download output</strong>, or <strong>Inspect in JSON Formatter</strong> to continue with the <a href="/json-formatter/">JSON formatter</a>. The JSON handoff keeps the raw text for inspection rather than automatically parsing and rewriting numeric IDs or duplicate keys.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Decompression does not guarantee JSON. The producer might have compressed XML, a log line, or another text format. The tool displays decoded text as supplied; it does not silently rename fields, reorder properties, or pretty-print the payload.</p>

  <h2>Open a .gz file, Base64URL value, or hex dump</h2>
  <p>If the evidence is a file rather than an API field, select <strong>Open file</strong> or drop a <code>.gz</code> file into the input panel. The browser reads its compressed bytes locally, displays an equivalent Base64 value, and decompresses it. No file upload or local bridge is needed. <strong>Download output</strong> saves the decompressed bytes; <strong>Download .gz</strong> saves the compressed stream.</p>
  <p>Auto input also accepts URL-safe Base64 using <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>, omitted <code>=</code> padding, and deliberate line wrapping. For a hex dump beginning with <code>1f8b</code>, leave Auto selected; otherwise choose Hex. The tool never guesses that a space was originally a plus sign. If transport damaged a value, return to the source.</p>
  <p>Binary or non-UTF-8 results are offered as a <code>.bin</code> download instead of being displayed with replacement characters. A <code>.tar.gz</code> becomes a tar file after GZip decompression, not an extracted folder: use an archive utility for that final step.</p>

  <h2>Check the sizes before copying the result</h2>
  <p>The result separates original bytes, compressed GZip bytes, and final Base64 size. Base64's transport overhead is not included in the GZip saving percentage. A short message can grow even when the compression is working correctly. When creating a payload, level 1 favors speed, level 6 is the balanced default, and level 9 spends more work on compression.</p>
  <p>Conversions run in a background worker. The page accepts up to 3 MiB of encoded input, 2 MiB of compressed data or text to compress, and 8 MiB of decompressed output. It stops after 8 seconds or at the output limit, keeping no partial success. Reduce a large capture to a representative message or use the <a href="/guides/gzip-base64-python-nodejs/">local script recipes</a> for larger jobs.</p>

  <h2>What does the H4sI prefix tell you?</h2>
  <p>A usual GZip stream begins with the bytes <code>1f 8b 08</code>: two identifying bytes followed by the DEFLATE compression-method byte. Their Base64 representation is <code>H4sI</code>. That makes the prefix a useful clue when examining a captured string. The header layout is defined in <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc1952/">RFC 1952</a>.</p>
  <p>A matching prefix is not an integrity check. A truncated copy can retain its first four characters and still fail to decompress. If the example above works but your payload does not, follow the <a href="/guides/fix-base64-gzip-decode-errors/">Base64 GZip troubleshooting guide</a>.</p>

  <h2>GZip and Base64 do different jobs</h2>
  <p>GZip is the compression layer. Base64 makes its binary result convenient to carry inside a text field. Neither step is encryption. The standard Base64 representation uses printable characters and may end with <code>=</code> padding; see <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc4648/">RFC 4648</a>.</p>
  <p>“Base64 GZip” describes the payload, but is an ambiguous instruction for creating one. To produce the format used here, start with text, encode it as UTF-8, compress those bytes with GZip, and Base64-encode the compressed result. The reverse-example link above follows that order.</p>

  <h2>Share an example someone else can reproduce</h2>
  <p>A useful question includes the encoded value, the result you expected, and the operation that failed. Add a runnable link beside that explanation so a reader can try it immediately. The text should remain understandable even without following the link.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Replace customer values or credentials with a small synthetic message <em>before</em> compressing it.</li>
    <li>Run the operation in Bug Days, then select <strong>Share</strong>.</li>
    <li>Choose the embedded-data link for an example that should not depend on short-link storage. Copy the link offered by the Share dialog.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Embedded links contain the sample itself; anyone with the link can read it. The separate short-link option stores a sample for 30 days. The worked examples on this page use embedded links, with no stored short-link record.</p>

  <h2>When to use a script instead</h2>
  <p>The browser is convenient for checking one message. For a repeated job, move the same steps into your application or a local script. Our <a href="/guides/gzip-base64-python-nodejs/">Python and Node.js recipes</a> use the standard libraries and include a Unicode example to catch character-encoding mistakes.</p>

